  backLabel="← Back to The Signature in the Swarm"
  title="They Made Him a Face"
  subtitle="The film sold as the finest picture of God breaks the doctrine it is selling."
  description="The Shack seats a Gnostic figure as judge, puts nail scars in the Father's wrists, and closes a murder with feeling. No law is broken, so no charge stands, so nothing is required and nothing is paid."
>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>The Shack</strong> — 2017, from the 2007 novel. Marketed to the Christian
    public as a breakthrough in understanding God.
  </p>

  <h2>What It Shows</h2>
  <p>
    Mack loses a daughter to a murderer and is invited back to the cabin where she died.
    There he meets three figures. Papa, the Father, appearing as a woman. Yahushua, a
    carpenter. And the Spirit, a woman named Sarayu — a river in India, taken out of
    Hindu scripture.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fourth figure named Sophia sits as judge. Sophia is the Gnostic construction, and
    the film seats her on a bench.
  </p>
  <p>Papa shows the nail scars in the wrists and says they were all there.</p>

  <h2>What It Teaches</h2>
  <ul class="roster">
    <li>No chain of command among the three. All equal in every direction, no submission anywhere.</li>
    <li>Sin is its own punishment, and Papa says He has no need to punish it.</li>
    <li>Forgiveness without repentance, without confession, without blood.</li>
    <li>No commandment appears in the film. No standard Mack is measured against.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Scripture Says</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>No likeness.</strong> Israel saw no manner of similitude at Horeb, and were
    told plainly that this was so they would not corrupt themselves by making one
    (Deuteronomy 4:15-16; Exodus 20:4). A bearded old man on a throne fails that test as
    surely as a woman does. This film was guilty at the casting call.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>And He named Himself.</strong> Father, King, Husband, He — thousands of
    times, never once the other way, in a world full of goddesses He never permitted His
    people to borrow. Mack had a bad father, so the film built him one that felt better.
    That is a god shaped to fit a wound, which is the same machinery as the golden calf
    under better lighting.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Son said the Father is greater than He (John 14:28). The head of Messiah is
    Yahuah (1 Corinthians 11:3). At the end the Son hands the kingdom back and is
    Himself subject (1 Corinthians 15:28). The film erases all three.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Then the wrists give it away.</strong> A Father who bleeds is not the
    Trinity. It is the Father suffering on the tree — the very thing the creed this film
    is selling condemns as heresy. The picture praised as the best explanation of the
    doctrine cannot state the doctrine correctly.
  </p>
  <p>
    And nothing is shed in that cabin. Without shedding of blood there is no remission
    (Hebrews 9:22). No law is broken, so no charge stands, so nothing is required and
    nothing is paid. The grief is closed by feeling, and the murderer is never answered
    for.
  </p>

  <h2>The Line</h2>
  <p>He told us what to call Him. Nobody asked for a rewrite.</p>

